00:00:05.235 --> 00:00:11.140
Today we will talk about algorithms for
finding regulatory motifs in DNA.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:17.420
And we will start from game when we will
be implanting patterns in random strings.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.270
Let's generate ten random sequences.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:25.460
And then let's take a 15-nucleotide-long
pattern, and insert it at

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.452
random positions in these ten sequences.
Now, turn your head,

00:00:30.452 --> 00:00:36.950
I will hide these patterns, and tell me
where these patterns are.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.979
What algorithm should you run to figure
out where I have inserted this pattern?

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.600
And you already probably recognized that if
we slightly modified our

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.860
frequent words problem, we will find the
pattern I have inserted.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.510
Indeed, you can concatenate all the
sequences and

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.530
find the most frequent word in the
resulting concatenate.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.970
The implanted pattern will appear ten
times in this concatenate.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.330
It is surprisingly frequent, which will most 
most likely mean

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.870
that it was the pattern that I have
inserted.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:20.640
Now let me change the experiment slightly.
Now instead of the

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.810
patterns that

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.890
I implant without any changes, 

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:30.360
I will insert the same 15 nucleotide long
patter with

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:37.150
four random mutations at random positions.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.548
And, in this is case, this pattern forms a
so-called (k,d) motif

00:01:42.548 --> 00:01:47.286
which is a k-mer that appears in every
sequence

00:01:47.286 --> 00:01:48.990
at most d mutations.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:55.430
Can you find such a pattern if I hide from
you where I inserted it?

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.720
Do you think that the Frequent Words Problem
will be able to help us?

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.670
Well, that all may be entertaining

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:08.400
but what biologists think
about this game?

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.630
I think biologists are falling asleep,
because it's absolutely

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:18.510
unclear what this problem has to do with
real biological problems.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.900
And I will try to show you that this
problem is

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:26.510
actually about a biological problem of
finding regulatory elements in DNA.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.940
My next question will be, do we have a
clock gene?

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.410
Think about our daily schedule

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.830
and think how our life depends on day and
night.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.995
In every cell, we probably have to express
different

00:02:44.995 --> 00:02:49.440
proteins depending on what time of the day
is it.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.010
But who are these molecular time keepers
who tell the

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.080
cells in our body what is the time right
now?

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.850
How do these molecular time keepers change gene expression to

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:07.489
produce proteins needed at night as
opposed to proteins needed in the morning?

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:14.690
And anoother questions that is relevant to
this, can we find genes responsible

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:20.490
for sleep disorders
When our Circadian Clock has problems.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:26.640
Now, we will focus on plants rather than
human because

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.120
for plants, keeping time is a matter of
life and death.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.230
Just think about photosynthesis,
flowering, or frost resistance.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.050
If you don't know what is the time, you
will be dead if you are a plant.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:46.860
And, the question that arises is
how probably a thousand plus

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.430
genes in plants follow the
circadian clock.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:52.100
Who controls them?

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.790
Who are the molecular managers who tells
these

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:58.770
genes to increase or decrease gene expression? 

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.040
Here comes the surprise.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.842
Just three genes in plants are molecular
managers that are

00:04:06.842 --> 00:04:12.140
responsible for orchestrating this 
circadian behavior.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:18.864
And they're called CCA1, LCY, and TOC1.
These genes are regulatory proteins,

00:04:18.864 --> 00:04:24.614
also know as transcription factors.
And these genes,

00:04:24.614 --> 00:04:25.460
to control other genes, 

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:31.020
(to exert control over circadian clock) 
they bind to short regions

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:37.200
(maybe 10 nucleotide, 15 nucleotides) in
the upstream regions of the genes.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.300
For example, if one of these regulatory

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:44.690
proteins wants to control a particular
circadian gene, 

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.400
it probably has a region within thousand
nucleotides

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.490
from the start of the gene where it binds.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.620
For example, here's CCA1, one of the three
regulators.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:02.490
and to exert control over these genes, it
has to bind in the upstream reason

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:07.830
of these genes.
But how does CCA1 know where to bind?

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:16.390
Probably there are some hidden messages
that tell CCA1: "Bind here!"

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:19.760
Our goal today is to find this particular
hidden

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.880
message (that's shown on the slide) where
CCA1 binds to.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:31.710
Of course, we don't know where
these hidden messages are actually hiding.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:36.140
In the upstream regions, and today we will
learn about algorithms

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:40.320
that are aimed at finding such hidden
messages.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.359
We will start from formulating the
problem.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.300
So, when we talked about implanting a
15-mer with four

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.980
random mutations, we figured out that the
Frequent Words Algorithm wouldn't be

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.390
able to find it, because there are no
frequent words

00:05:56.390 --> 00:06:02.190
that appearing without mutations in the
resulting strings after implantations.

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:06.320
That's why we need to solve a different
problem that I call the

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.670
Implanted Motif Problem.
In this problem, you are given a set of

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:18.990
stings Dna, and integers k and d.
And you need to find all (k,d) motifs in Dna.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.630
How should we solve this problem?

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:28.045
Should we possibly explore all possible 4
to the power k k-mers and

00:06:28.045 --> 00:06:34.060
see which of them represent (k,d)-motifs.
That will take time.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.290
That's why, let's try something else.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.280
Let's try to see whether a pairwise
comparison

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.330
(comparing sequences to each other) would
help.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:43.460
Think about this.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.056
Each sequence is random.

00:06:45.056 --> 00:06:48.590
The only non-randomness in these sequences
is

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:53.740
are these implanted motifs, and that's why they
probably exhibit

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:58.260
larger similarity than other regions of
random sequences.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.968
Thus, my idea, let's find the most.

00:07:00.968 --> 00:07:01.744
closed 15-mers,

00:07:01.744 --> 00:07:02.750
[INAUDIBLE],

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:05.680
let's say in the first and second
sequence,

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:12.290
and it will give us an idea on how the
implanted pattern looks like.

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:13.320
Would it work?

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.880
Let's figure out whether the pairwise
comparison between

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.689
strings will help us to find the implanted
motifs.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:28.290
Well, unfortunately, it won't work because
when we implant a pattern, it has

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:33.970
four differences from the original pattern,
but we don't have 

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:37.920
access to the pattern, so there is
nothing to compare with.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:40.960
The only thing that we have are implanted
instances.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.820
But every two implanted instances actually

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:48.840
may be four plus four mutations
apart.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.580
How we can find them?
Since pairwise comparison won't work,

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.510
maybe the only option we have is just to

00:07:56.510 --> 00:08:00.212
explore all four to the power k possible
k-mers.

00:08:00.212 --> 00:08:01.490
.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.160
Should we explore all of them?

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:09.110
Not necessarily, because if a k-mer is so
far away from all k-mers

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:13.010
in the strings that we analyze, there is
no reason to explore it.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:16.180
It cannot possibly be an implanted
pattern.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:18.600
That's why motif enumeration algorithm
would

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:19.880
look like this.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:23.760
We will start from each k-mer, from a
sequence.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.330
Let's call it a.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:30.480
For each such k-mer, let generate all
possible mutations with up to

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.770
d nucleotides mutated, and for each such
mutated k-mer

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:41.186
a', let's check whether it appears as
a (k,d)-mer in the string.

00:08:41.186 --> 00:08:43.230
That will work,

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:44.920
 

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:50.250
(it will be slow but definitely will work if k is small)

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:55.710
but the question we should ask, would it
solve the real biological problem?

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.420
Unfortunately it won't, it won't work
because our

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.820
model for implanting patterns is not very
biological.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.550
It doesn't properly reflect biological
reality.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:08.260
I would say a little bit

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:12.340
messy biological reality, because when
biologist generate,

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.140
let's say, a set of genes, that are

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:18.820
controlled by circadian clock, they cannot
guarantee that

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:22.850
all these genes have a particular pattern
implanted.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:28.220
Some genes don't have any patterns
implanted in their upstream region.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.520
As a result, we need to find out a way to
search for motifs, even if

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.110
some sequence don't have any motifs
implanted.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:43.250
And that makes our life, little bit more
difficult and we need to develop scoring

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:48.900
for every set of motifs, even in the case
when some of the implanted pattern

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:54.390
don't look very similar to the canonical
motif.

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:58.040
And we'll move to the next topic, which is
motif finding problem

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:58.850
that is more

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:02.880
adequate for finding regulatory
motifs.

